reviewer_comments.reviewer_3:
In the present manuscript, C. Lai, K. Hou and colleagues identified Olig2 as barrier during in vivo direct reprogramming of astrocytes into neurons. First, the authors showed that Olig2 is upregulated in astrocytes following the expression of bHLH TFs. Then, they investigated the effect of Olig2 knock-down in Ngn2-mediated direct conversion, unravelling an increased reprogramming efficiency and maturation of Ngn2-shOlig2-iNs; last, they performed single cell RNAseq analysis to investigate the mechanisms regulated by (sh)Olig2 in the conversion process.
Overall the manuscript is interesting and of interest for the in vivo direct reprogramming field.
However, there are several points that need to addressed before considering for publication;
_ the identification of Olig2 as barrier for in vivo direct reprogramming was postulated and partially revealed years ago (doi/10.1073/pnas.0506535102). While the present manuscript is a substantial step forward towards understanding the mechanisms underlying the conversion process, the role of Olig2 in direct neuronal reprogramming is not “unexplored” (line 160). The authors are well-aware of the paper, as they used the same approach to manipulate Olig2 (using Olig2-VP16). Therefore, it is important to reshape the introduction to include previous work on Olig2;
_ Previous work from the same group showed that the proportion of astrocytes expressing Olig2 in vivo is region-specific, spanning from 8% to 13% in the cortex (doi.org/10.1186/s13041-021-00747-0). However, in their control-injected mice (Fig.1f, h), the proportion of GFP+ cells expressing Olig2 is very low. Can the authors comment on the discrepancy of the results? Is it possible that their AAVs or Gfap promoter have a preference for a subset of astrocytes?
_ Can the authors quantify the intensity of Olig2 in OPCs following control and Ngn2 transduction (figure 1f)?. According to the figure, it seems that in Ngn2-injection condition, tdT+GFP- cells have a stronger signal than tdT+GFP- cells in control condition; that would suggest a cell non-autonomous effect;
_ the authors quantified the number of Iba1+ cells and did not find a change in different conditions; can the authors comment and discuss the morphology of Iba1+ cells, to better understand whether the presence of the reprogramming factor leads to an inflammatory state?
_ Please specify in the text and figures when transgenics mice and when viruses are used. For instance, in figure 1k it is not clear that the authors used tdT comes from the AAV injection and not from the Sox10-CreERT2::Ai14 transgenic mouse used in experiment in panel 1f;
_ can the authors include as well GFP channel in Figure 2C, to better understand the proportion of infected cells with the sole AAV-shNC/Olig2? Can the authors quantify the proportion of double-infected cells (tdT+GFP+). See comment in the scRNASeq;
_ Supplementary figure 2 is missing, at least in the downloaded pdf file;
_ the observation that cells at intermediate stage are increased in Ngn2-shOlig2 condition is very interesting, but is not followed up;
- can the authors test whether these cells are still expressing low levels of Olig2? Or do they express higher levels of neural stem cells markers (e.g., Sox2, Pax6)? Can the authors find them in their scRNAseq? what is their signature?
- please specify that the quantification of the intensity in suppl. Fig. 4e refers to supply. Fig. 4d and suppl. Fig.4g refers to Suppl. Fig.4f;
_ scRNAseq data are interesting and raise some important questions:
- Supplementary figure 7e shows the distribution of TdT+ cells in various clusters; if correctly interpreted, suppl. Fig. 7s shows the proportion of TdT+ cells in various cluster as well, providing quantitative information to Supplement. Fig. 7e. From these 2 panels, it is evident that at least 25% of TdT+ cells are located in the oligodendrocyte lineage. However, in the initial characterisation of the vector system, the authors claim that the system is very tight and specific for astrocytes. Can the authors explain this discrepancy? Which of the data is true? Can the authors analyze as well these “oligodendrocyte” cluster and compare it to the “astrocyte” cluster of infected cells to get an insight whether a neuronal cascade is being induced in oligodendrocytes or not?
_ As the authors use 2 different AAV vectors, one carrying TF-TdT and one shControl/Olig2-GFP, it is very likely the presence of 3 groups of infected cells: single one (TdT+, GFP+) and double positive (tdT+GFP+). Can the authors detect these 3 groups in the scRNAseq?
_ If so, can the authors show in which clusters the various subgroups are localized? Do Ngn2-tdT+ cells coming from Ngn2-shControl localize together with Ngn2-shControl and Ngn2 only from Ngn2-shOlig2?
_ Are Ngn2-shOlig2 double positive cells (and not just tdT+ cells) enriched in astrocyte cluster 4 (Fig.4n)? It is not clear whether the authors subset only the double positive astrocytes, or they consider all astrocytes coming from the injection, irrespective of whether they are single, double or not transduced at all. A clarification in the text and figure will greatly improve the interpretation of the data;
_ the inclusion of NPCs into the data allows for a better interpretation of the data; however, it is not clear the source of the NPCs. Did the author sequence as well NPCs? Did they merge the data from another publication? This information could not be found in the material and method;
_ can the authors confirm via IF the change in the expression of at least 1 gene coding for mitochondrial protein? This would substantiate the interesting finding.
_ The interpretation that the increase in Ngn2 depends on the derepression of the endogenous Ngn2 promoter in shOlig 2 conditions is difficult to prove. As the authors stain for Ngn2, can the authors discriminate between the endogenous Ngn2 and the over-expressed? It has previously been shown that Olig2 can regulate Gfap expression (doi:10.1242/dev.02847). A possibility is that Olig2 regulates the Gfap promoter used to induce the expression of Ngn2 in AAV. Can the authors exclude this hypothesis by testing it? For instance by using a luciferase assay?
_ In the discussion, the authors suggest that Olig2 activation might be an “second line of defence against bHLH proneural transcription factor mediate cell reprogramming” (lines 623-624) based on the the fact that Sox2 or Dlx2 did not induce Olig2. This sounds a kind of deterministic hypothesis, where cells have “implemented” the possibility that bHLH-mediate cell reprogramming occurs and it needs to be prevented. However, it is well known that upon injury or neurodegeneration, astrocytes become Olig2 positive. Therefore, it seems more likely that Olig2 induction reflects some sort of reactive state. Can the authors check if shOlig2+ cells show lower level of reactivity in scRNAseq and via IF? Either case, the cited sentence needs to be changed.
